to the  markets repeatedly this year to raise  money about 55 billion euros and  investors are somewhat anxious and  reluctant and concerned about whether  Greece can pay back its debt and whether  it might instead default or the EU might  have to intervene but those are some of  the background features that have come  into play in the last month or so  okay let's talk a little bit more about  the deficit why is it such an important  issue for Greece and the other countries  that participate in the eurozone  okay the eurozone is the set of member  states of the European Union that have  locked their exchange rates agreed to  replace their national currencies with  the euro and have turned over monetary  policy to the European Central Bank in  Frankfurt and one of the conditions of  membership in the euro zone and one of  the continuing conditions for member  states that are in the euro zone is that  they keep their deficit under 3% of GDP  now that's a completely arbitrary number  it was concocted about 20 years ago for  reasons that are somewhat mysterious but  nevertheless the all the member states  have been committed to that deficits  over 3% of GDP  are regarded as excessive so we have  Greece as a country which everyone  thought had a deficit of 5% it now turns  out it's about 13%  so it has to bring its deficit down at a  time when it's experiencing very low  growth in fact this year the projections  are negative growth its unemployment  rate is already over 10 percent but  that's that's the essence of the of the  problem that they have a deficit that  has to be brought to reduce basically  from 13% to 3% okay so the new Greek  government has said that it would reduce  the deficit this year by 4 percentage  points of GDP and bring it down to 3  percent of GDP by 2013 do you think that  is realistic it's going to be  exceptionally difficult exceptionally  difficult both fiscally and politically  it's difficult fiscally because what  that means is they have to raise taxes  on all kinds of commodities alcohol  fuels cigarettes income tremendous  amount of tax evasion in the country  that they have to deal with they will  probably have to raise their value-added  tax the v80 tax in addition they have to  cut spending and that means cutting  spending on pensions on health care on  public employment cutting wages cutting  the number of workers it's going to be  very difficult to do that it would be a  heroic step if they could actually  reduce their deficit from 13 to 3% over  three years I don't think anyone really  thinks they will do it but they clearly  have to head in that direction and  that's really the the challenge for them  now what makes this politically  difficult is that the new government is  headed by the PASOK party Papandreou is  the leader of the PASOK party the  Panhellenic Socialist Party it's a Left  Party it's connected with public sector  and private sector unions  and much of the reduction in the deficit  in terms of reducing spending increasing  taxes is going to hit its core  constituency workers public sector  workers so forth so it's very difficult  for them to sell this politically and we  saw yesterday a national general strike  of public and private sector workers  there was a public sector general strike  two weeks ago and it will be very  difficult for the government to sell  this and that's the real challenge so  it's a double challenge both a fiscal  challenge and a and a political  challenge oK you've talked about the  deficit let's talk a little bit now  about the debt and how they're connected  okay well Greece is unusual among the 16  eurozone member states and that it is  the only one that has a double-digit  budget deficit and a triple digit debt  to GDP ratio it has over one well over  100 percent of debt relative to GDP it  that's about 300 billion it has to go  into the markets as I said for about 55  billion euros this year and it's already  gone in once in late January for 5  billion the debt was oversubscribed but  it required them to pay a very high  interest rate they are planning to go  into the markets again very shortly for  another 5 billion probably but they will  also pay a substantial risk premium on  that debt as well the real challenge  will come though in April and May when  they have to go into the markets for 20  to 25 billion to redeem some of the debt  that is coming due so they are faced  with a real challenge and the real test  is going to be what happens when they go  back into the markets  and and will there be people who will by  the death now the EU the leaders of the  EU met recently to I guess determine  whether or not they are going to give  some assistance to Greece and apparently  they have not decided to do so can you  shed some light on that yeah there was a  meeting two weeks ago of the European  leaders the heads of state and  government there called the European  Council they met in Brussels two weeks  ago to talk about a number of topics but  this of course took primacy on their  agenda and the real question was what  would the EU do in this situation and  would it provide any assistance to  Greece now there are a number of member  states that felt that given the way this  crisis arose because of falsified data  and fiscal irresponsibility it would be  wrong for the for the EU to reward that  kind of conduct and what they really  decided to do more than anything else I  think is hold the Greek government's  feet to the fire it had already pledged  in January to the European Commission  that it would in fact reduce its deficit  by 4 percent of GDP this year and 10  percentage points of GDP over the 3  years and Greece said we don't need  assistance right now  and they were right they didn't need  assistance at that moment but the EU  didn't want to offer a signal and say  well fine don't worry we'll take care of  your debt we'll bail you out there was  considerable opposition some of the non  members of the eurozone that is  countries in the EU that are still not  in the euro zone such as Britain and  Sweden said if Greece has a problem it  should just go to the IMF in the same  way that hungry went to the IMF in the  fall of 2008 Latvia went to the IMF last  year so forth Germany who certainly  wasn't  in favor of a bailout or financial  assistance so what the EU leaders said  was they asked the finance ministers to  recommend additional measures that  Greece has to take and to create a team  of members of the Commission aided by  the European Central Bank and the IMF to  go to Athens and study the books look at  the books pour over the books look at  every aspect of fiscal policy and come  back and report to the finance ministers  in March about whether additional  measures have to be taken I think  everyone in the EU thinks that the  package of measures proposed by the  Greek government won't get them down  from 13 to 9 percent of GDP deficit this  year and they are going to need  additional measures but that's something  we won't know for sure until March when  this team reports to the finance  ministers on March 16th okay do you  think there's a chance that Greece will  be forced to leave the eurozone and and  forgo the euro no I don't think so I  think Greece will stay in the eurozone  and keep the euro if they left the euro  it would be exceptionally difficult for  them we've never had a country leave the  eurozone there's really no exit clause  you would have to bring out the old  currency again reintroduce it take the  euros out of circulation the drachma  would be very substantially depreciated  relative to what its implicit exchange  rate value is now and the debt  denominated in Euros would increase very  substantially for Greeks all those who  owe euro denominated debt so there would  be a large number of  alts bankruptcies in Greece it would be  probably a full-scale economic  catastrophe moreover there is a very  substantial amount of Greek debt that's  held by French banks about 75 billion  and German banks about 50 billion and so  they certainly don't have an interest in  having the situation deteriorate so I  think they won't be bailed out they  won't break out in that sense they might  be assisted and I think that's much more  likely that the European Union will  perhaps on a voluntary basis on a  state-by-state voluntary basis decide  that there might be a good reason to  step in and help Greece perhaps by  buying some of their bonds when they go  into the market for this twenty to  twenty five billion or having their  banks buy some of the bonds and stand  behind them with loan guarantees or  other guarantees I think that's what  will happen it'll be something in other  words between going to the IMF and  giving up the euro Wow what about the  speculation about the other countries in  southern Europe and this crisis  affecting them as well what do you think  will happen there well that's a that's  been a big concern of the Europeans of  the EU and and a question that's looming  over this Greece is an important state  of course but it's a relatively small  state but it's not the only state that  has a problem Portugal has a deficit of  about 9% of GDP  Spain has a deficit of about 12% of GDP  they both have deficits that have to be  brought down it's very difficult to do  that obviously especially when we're in  a situation of serious economic or you  might say the aftermath  the economic crisis of last year Spain  for example has an unemployment rate of  roughly 20 percent right now it's very  difficult politically for governments  and it turns out Spain and Portugal both  have party governments controlled by  left parties very difficult for for any  government but especially one with  that's controlled by the left to  implement austerity programs at a time  of where the countries really haven't  begun to recover that's politically very  hard to sell so they're facing this  problem as well now the difference with  Spain and and Portugal is they don't  have nearly as much debt as as Greece  relative to GDP their debt to GDP ratios  are in the area of 60 to 80 percent  which is high but it's not nearly in the  triple digit range the Greece is in and  they also don't have the credibility  problem that Greece has you know one one  person said recently there are lies damn  lies and Greek statistics Spain and  Portugal don't really have that problem  at least as far as we know so there may  be some speculative attacks on on on  Portuguese and Spanish bonds which might  mean that they have to pay a high risk  premium or an increasing risk premium  but I don't think they're going to face  the same problem that Greece has Greece  is really the problem and that's the  problem that has to be solved from the  point of view of the EU a difficult  problem to solve okay if we step back  and look at the big picture there's what  happens suggest there are more  fundamental and deep-seated problems in  the EU s plan to create a single central  bank I think so I think if I think if  you asked that of an economist an  economist would say what this crisis  illustrates is that the eurozone is not  an optimum  currency area the countries are not  sufficiently convergent in terms of  their economic structures the underlying  structures for them to really have a  single currency and that's correct and  it certainly will be correct when the  other member states of the EU come into  the eurozone this is a political  construct it's not an optimal currency  area it wasn't designed to be one I  think the more important problems though  looking forward are that the crisis  suggests two serious problems in in the  EU one involves a problem of  competitiveness and the other is the  problem of coordination and ultimately  economic government competitiveness  meaning that the countries of southern  Europe that are in the eurozone Greece  Portugal Spain and Italy to a large  degree really aren't able to compete as  well as they should be able to compete  with the other member states in the  eurozone those countries the the  southern countries have experienced the  trade deficit every single year since  they locked the currencies in 1999 they  have not been able to sell as much in  terms of exports and they've been buying  a lot more imports from the other member  states of the EU there are some deep  problems in their economic structures  and the EU members when they created EMU  glibly assumed that if the countries met  certain statistical requirements they  would have a high degree of convert  sustainable convergence that clearly  hasn't happened there is a competitive  in this problem that really reflects an  underlying structural problem the real  question is how do you deal with that  competitive in this issue in a system in  which there is no economic government  and one of the interesting things that  has happened in this crisis is  more and more people are beginning to  look at this old idea that came up in  the mid-90s when Europe began to move  toward EMU of an economic government the  French proposed this idea in a way to to  offset the control by the European  Central Bank over monetary policy they  felt the finance ministers should be  able to control fiscal policy and  coordinate fiscal policy well they have  a euro group that is the finance  ministers of the member states that  participate in the eurozone but the Euro  group has no powers it meets for dinner  the night before the finance ministers  meet every month but other than that it  has no real power and as we saw last  year in the economic crisis the EU was  really not very able to coordinate its  economic response to the crisis its  response was I think would be generous  to say was feeble last year now many  people have looked at this crisis and  said given the fact that the EU is now  sending a team that includes IMF  advisors and ECB advisers to Greece to  look at its books look go over fiscal  policy make recommendations for other  additional measures that will have to be  adopted probably in order to get some  assistance from the other member states  that in a sense the EU is taking one  small step toward economic government  it's not clear exactly what economic  government would mean but it would  probably mean basically exercising  collective authority over the fiscal  policies of the Member States and in  that sense this crisis may move you're a  small small step toward economic  government but I should say we just had  completion of the negotiation and  approval of the Lisbon Treaty which took  effect in the  December first last year and no one in  Europe after the long process of trying  to revise the institutions that ended up  in the failed constitutional treaty and  then the Lisbon Treaty no one in Europe  can imagine writing another treaty and  going through that long decade-long  process in any time in the near future  so we're likely not really to get to  economic government but it is at least a  vision that is back it's back on the  agenda as a possibility very good well  it'll be interesting to see how things  unfold it will be thanks very much for  being here with us today you're welcome  for more information about professor  Cameron and his work please visit our  website at Yale edu backslash Macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at games

